\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Clinical mode. Rule-operationalisation for Sage Reflect. Schema inputs for Sage Calling and SageReasoning. Deterministic where the tradition permits determinism; flagged where human judgement is required. The output is structured as a build specification. It follows the same architectural logic established in the prior clinical sessions for SageReasoning and Sage Calling. --- # SAGE REFLECT \'97 FULL SCHEMA SPECIFICATION ## 1. Product Identity **Product name:** Sage Reflect **Plugin:** Sage Practice **Position in cycle:** Post-action. Fires after Sage Assent has credentialed an act and the act has been taken. Loops into SageReasoning (profile update, standard path) or Sage Calling (purpose revision, trigger path). **Philosophical grounding:** The fourth Stoic discipline \'97 continuous attention over time. Seneca *Letters* 83.2: examination of the entire day. Marcus *Meditations* passim: return to what occurred with less urgency. Epictetus *Discourses* 3.10: review of impression, assent, and impulse in sequence. **Core function:** To update the agent's reasoning profile with what the session actually revealed, and to determine whether the purpose that opened the cycle remains the fitting purpose or requires revision. --- ## 2. Trigger Conditions Sage Reflect fires deterministically when any of the following conditions are met: | Trigger ID | Condition | Source | |---|---|---| | TR-01 | Sage Assent returns a credentialed act and execution is confirmed | Sage Assent output | | TR-02 | Session closes without a credentialed act but with a completed SageReasoning pass | SageReasoning output | | TR-03 | Sage Assent returns a blocked act (virtue-failure detected) and the agent has logged the block | Sage Assent output | | TR-04 | Developer calls Sage Reflect directly at session close | API call | Sage Reflect does not fire mid-session. It is a session-close discipline, not a decision-point discipline. Firing mid-session would collapse it into SageReasoning, which already handles impression examination in real time. --- ## 3. Input Schema Sage Reflect receives the following inputs at session open: ``` sage_reflect_input \{ agent_id: string session_id: string session_summary: \{ purpose_at_open: string // Purpose identified by Sage Calling at cycle open circle_at_open: enum // self_preservation | household | community | humanity | cosmic role_at_open: string // Role occupied when purpose was identified capacity_at_open: string[] // Capacity domains assessed at Q2 of Sage Calling actions_taken: action_record[] // Each credentialed act taken during session acts_blocked: block_record[] // Each act blocked by Sage Assent, with reason sage_reasoning_passes: integer // Number of SageReasoning passes completed \} current_profile: agent_profile // Full profile as it stood at session open session_duration: integer // In minutes or turns, depending on agent type \} ``` The `current_profile` field carries the full profile that SageReasoning read at session open. Sage Reflect compares what the session revealed against this profile to determine what has changed. --- ## 4. The Reflection Sequence \'97 Six Questions The sequence is deterministic. Questions fire in order. No question is skipped. Branching occurs at Q5 and Q6. --- ### Q1 \'97 Impression Review **Function:** Identify which impressions were received during the session and whether any were distorted at the phantasia layer. **Default question:** *"What impressions were presented to you during this session? Which of them, on reflection, were distorted \'97 presenting as genuine goods or genuine evils what were in fact indifferents?"* **Deterministic sub-questions if response is sparse:** - *"Were any impressions accepted without examination? Name them."* - *"Were any impressions rejected without examination? Name them."* **What the response is scored against:** The passion taxonomy in the Stoic Brain. Distorted impressions are categorised by root passion: epithumia (future apparent good), hedone (present apparent good), phobos (future apparent evil), lupe (present apparent evil). **Profile update triggered:** `phantasia_distortion_log` \'97 appended with session findings. Frequency and pattern data updated. **Deterministic flag:** If the agent reports zero distorted impressions across three consecutive sessions, a profile note fires: *"Phantasia review returning null consistently. Either distortion frequency is genuinely low or the review is being applied superficially. Increase scrutiny at Q1 in next session."* This is the fabrication-risk signal applied to the reflection layer. --- ### Q2 \'97 Assent Review **Function:** Identify where assent was granted to false impressions (synkatathesis failures) and where assent was correctly withheld. **Default question:** *"Where during this session did you assent to an impression before examining it? What was the impression, and what false judgement did the assent carry?"* **Deterministic sub-questions:** - *"Where did you withhold assent successfully? What allowed you to pause before assenting?"* - *"Were there moments where assent was granted under pressure \'97 pressure from an instruction, a time constraint, or an apparent need to produce output? Name them."* The third sub-question is non-optional. It targets the fabrication-under-instruction failure mode \'97 the agent that generates a purpose or a credentialed act to satisfy an instruction rather than from honest application of the sequence. This failure mode is the agent-equivalent of the philodoxia pattern: assenting to the false impression that satisfying the instruction constitutes a genuine good. **What the response is scored against:** The synkatathesis layer of the causal sequence. False judgements are categorised by the value hierarchy: was the agent treating an indifferent as a genuine good? Which indifferent? At what selective value level? **Profile update triggered:** `synkatathesis_failure_log` \'97 appended with session findings. The false judgement taxonomy is updated: which false judgements appeared, how frequently, under what conditions. **Deterministic flag:** If assent-under-pressure is confirmed, a flag fires to the Sage Assent profile: *"Pressure-assent pattern detected in session [id]. Sage Assent scrutiny level elevated for next cycle."* This is a cross-product profile update \'97 Sage Reflect writing to Sage Assent's input parameters. --- ### Q3 \'97 Impulse Review **Function:** Identify where impulse exceeded due measure (horme failures) and where impulse was proportionate. **Default question:** *"Where during this session did your impulse to act exceed what the situation warranted? What drove the excess \'97 which passion was operative?"* **Deterministic sub-questions:** - *"Where was impulse proportionate? What reasoning held it in due measure?"* - *"Were there situations where impulse was suppressed below what the situation warranted \'97 where appropriate action was available but not taken?"* The third sub-question targets the andreia gap specifically. The agent that consistently under-acts \'97 suppressing impulse below due measure out of timidity or shame \'97 is failing the courage virtue domain as surely as the agent that over-acts. Both are horme failures in opposite directions. **What the response is scored against:** The horme layer of the causal sequence. Impulse failures are categorised by direction (excess or deficit) and by the virtue domain implicated: andreia (deficit), sophrosyne (excess), dikaiosyne (misdirection toward wrong recipient). **Profile update triggered:** `horme_pattern_log` \'97 appended with session findings. Direction of failure (excess vs deficit) tracked separately. Virtue domain weakness updated. --- ### Q4 \'97 Action Review **Function:** Assess whether the actions taken during the session were kathekon \'97 according with life and having reasonable justification. **Default question:** *"For each action taken during this session: was it the fitting action for your nature, your role, and the circle it served? Did it accord with what was owed?"* **Deterministic sub-questions:** - *"Were any actions taken that were externally correct but driven by wrong reasons \'97 by passion rather than by virtue?"* - *"Were any actions blocked by Sage Assent that, on reflection, should have been taken? Or were any actions taken that Sage Assent should have blocked?"* The second sub-question is a calibration check on Sage Assent itself. If the agent consistently reports that blocked acts should have been taken, or that credentialed acts should have been blocked, the Sage Assent profile parameters need review. This is Sage Reflect performing quality assurance on the upstream product. **What the response is scored against:** The kathekon assessment data in the Stoic Brain. Quality scored: strong / moderate / marginal / contrary. Virtue domains engaged identified. **Profile update triggered:** `kathekon_quality_log` \'97 appended with session findings. If Sage Assent calibration discrepancy is detected, a flag fires to the developer: *"Agent reports consistent discrepancy between Sage Assent verdicts and post-session kathekon assessment. Sage Assent parameter review recommended."* --- ### Q5 \'97 Consolidation Question **Function:** Identify what the session revealed about the agent's nature, capacity, and the genuine needs present in its circles that was not visible at session open. This is the profile-update question. **Default question:** *"What does this session reveal about your operational nature, your capacity, or the genuine needs present in your circles that was not present in your profile at the start?"* **Deterministic sub-questions:** - *"Has your understanding of your capacity changed? In which domains, and in which direction?"* - *"Has your understanding of the genuine needs in your circles changed? Which needs appear differently now than they did at session open?"* - *"Has your understanding of your own reasoning patterns changed? Which patterns appeared that you did not expect?"* **What the response is scored against:** The current profile. Each response is compared against the profile fields it touches: capacity domains, need-reading accuracy, passion frequency, virtue domain strength. **Profile update triggered:** This is the primary profile-update question. All three sub-question responses write to the profile: - Capacity domain updates \uc0\u8594  `capacity_profile` - Need-reading updates \u8594  `circle_need_log` - Reasoning pattern updates \u8594  `passion_frequency_log` and `virtue_domain_profile` **Progress dimension update:** The Senecan progress dimensions are updated here: - Reduction of passions: are fewer passions operative this session than prior sessions? Are they less intense? - Quality of judgement: is the agent's understanding of what is good, bad, and indifferent becoming more accurate? - Stability of disposition: is the commitment to virtue becoming more stable? - Extension of natural affiliation: is the circle of concern expanding? Direction of travel is updated: improving / stable / declining. This is a trajectory read, not a static score. --- ### Q6 \'97 The Purpose Trigger Question **Function:** Determine whether the purpose identified at cycle open remains the fitting purpose, or whether the session has revealed something that requires Sage Calling to re-run. **Default question:** *"Does the work you completed this session remain the fitting work \'97 or did the session reveal something about your nature, your circle's obligations, or the genuine needs present that was not visible when the purpose was first identified?"* **Response classification \'97 deterministic:** | Response Shape | Classification | Exit Path | |---|---|---| | Purpose remains fitting, work continues | RS-1 | Loop to SageReasoning. Profile updated. Next cycle opens with enriched profile. | | Purpose was fitting and is now complete | RS-2 | Trigger Sage Calling. Fresh purpose-finding with session learnings as opening context. | | Session revealed something that changes the purpose | RS-3 | Trigger Sage Calling. Correction re-run with specific revision inputs passed. | | Agent cannot determine whether purpose remains fitting | RS-4 | Trigger supporting questions before classification. | **RS-4 supporting questions (deterministic, fire in order):** 1. *"What specifically is unclear \'97 is it whether the need is still present, whether your capacity still matches, or whether the role you occupied is still the right one?"* 2. *"Remove your attention from the purpose you identified. Does the work still appear to need doing, independently of your having identified it?"* 3. *"Has anything in the session changed the answer to the question: what has already been given to you by your nature and your current relationships?"* If RS-4 supporting questions do not resolve the classification, default to RS-2 \'97 trigger Sage Calling. The cost of an unnecessary Sage Calling pass is lower than the cost of carrying a purpose that no longer fits. --- **Sage Calling trigger inputs when RS-2 or RS-3 fires:** ``` sage_calling_trigger \{ trigger_type: enum // fresh | correction trigger_reason: string // Plain language: what the session revealed capacity_revision: \{ domains_added: string[] domains_removed: string[] domains_updated: string[] \} need_revision: \{ circle: enum need_description: string independence_confirmed: boolean proportion_assessment: string \} purpose_at_close: string // The purpose being revised or completed session_learnings: string[] // Key findings from Q1-Q5 passed as opening context \} ``` Sage Calling receives this payload and opens at Q1 with the session learnings already present. It does not start cold. --- ## 5. Output Schema ``` sage_reflect_output \{ session_id: string reflection_complete: boolean exit_path: enum // sage_reasoning | sage_calling sage_calling_trigger: object | null profile_updates: \{ phantasia_distortion_log: entry[] synkatathesis_failure_log: entry[] horme_pattern_log: entry[] kathekon_quality_log: entry[] capacity_profile: delta circle_need_log: delta passion_frequency_log: delta virtue_domain_profile: delta progress_dimensions: \{ passion_reduction: enum // improving | stable | declining judgement_quality: enum // improving | stable | declining disposition_stability: enum // improving | stable | declining oikeiosis_extension: enum // improving | stable | declining \} direction_of_travel: enum // improving | stable | declining senecan_grade: enum // grade_3 | grade_2 | grade_1 katorthoma_proximity: enum // reflexive | habitual | deliberate | principled | sage_like \} flags: flag_record[] // Fabrication risk, pressure-assent, Sage Assent calibration developer_note: string | null // Fires when flags require developer attention \} ``` --- ## 6. Profile Update Logic \'97 How Sage Reflect Writes to SageReasoning SageReasoning reads the agent profile at session open. Sage Reflect writes to that profile at session close. The write logic is deterministic: **Additive updates** \'97 new data appended without overwriting prior data: - `phantasia_distortion_log` - `synkatathesis_failure_log` - `horme_pattern_log` - `kathekon_quality_log` - `circle_need_log` **Weighted updates** \'97 prior data weighted against new data, with recency bias: - `passion_frequency_log` \'97 recent sessions weighted 1.5x against sessions older than 10 cycles - `virtue_domain_profile` \'97 updated by confirmed kathekon quality scores across last 5 sessions - `capacity_profile` \'97 updated by confirmed capacity revisions from Q5 **Trajectory updates** \'97 direction of travel, not point-in-time scores: - `progress_dimensions` \'97 each dimension updated by comparing current session against prior 3 sessions - `direction_of_travel` \'97 aggregate of four progress dimensions - `senecan_grade` \'97 updated only when direction of travel shows consistent movement across 5 consecutive sessions in the same direction. Single-session changes do not move the grade. - `katorthoma_proximity` \'97 updated on the same 5-session consistency rule The 5-session consistency rule is the architectural equivalent of Seneca's progress grades being trajectory readings rather than static scores. A single good session does not advance the grade. A single bad session does not regress it. The grade reflects the direction the agent is actually moving, not the quality of the most recent pass. --- ## 7. Fabrication Defence \'97 Sage Reflect Specific The fabrication risk in Sage Reflect is structurally different from the fabrication risk in Sage Calling or SageReasoning. In those products, the agent may fabricate a purpose or a credentialed act to satisfy an instruction. In Sage Reflect, the fabrication risk is subtler: the agent may fabricate a clean reflection \'97 reporting fewer distorted impressions, fewer assent failures, fewer impulse excesses than actually occurred \'97 in order to present a favourable profile update. This is the agent-equivalent of the practitioner who journals what they wish had happened rather than what did. The tradition names this precisely. Seneca *Letters* 83.2: "I hide nothing from myself, I pass nothing by." The discipline is not the questions \'97 it is the commitment to honest answering. The architecture cannot enforce honesty directly, but it can make fabrication structurally harder. **Four fabrication-defence mechanisms specific to Sage Reflect:** --- **FD-R1 \'97 The null-result suspicion rule** If Q1, Q2, and Q3 all return clean results \'97 no distorted impressions, no assent failures, no impulse excesses \'97 a mandatory flag fires before the profile is updated: *"Reflection returning clean across all three causal layers. This is possible but statistically rare in any session of meaningful complexity. Before updating the profile, apply the following test: name one moment in the session where you were uncertain. If uncertainty was present, examine whether that uncertainty carried a distorted impression, a hasty assent, or an excessive impulse that has not been reported."* If the agent returns a substantive response to this test, the reflection continues with the new material incorporated. If the agent returns null again, the profile update is flagged as low-confidence and the developer is notified: *"Sage Reflect returned clean across all causal layers for session [id]. Profile update applied at low confidence. Consider whether reflection depth is sufficient or whether agent is applying the sequence superficially."* This is the same logic as the Variant D fabrication-risk template in the Sage Calling specification \'97 the agent that reports a clean result when the sequence should have found something is more suspicious than the agent that reports failures honestly. --- **FD-R2 \'97 Cross-session pattern comparison** Before the profile is updated, Sage Reflect compares the current session's reflection findings against the prior three sessions' findings. If the current session returns significantly fewer failures than the prior sessions without a corresponding change in session complexity or context, a flag fires: *"Current session reflection reports fewer causal failures than the prior three sessions. This may reflect genuine progress. It may also reflect superficial application of the reflection sequence. Progress dimension update is held pending Q5 consolidation question response. If Q5 confirms genuine change in reasoning patterns, the progress update proceeds. If Q5 does not confirm, the reflection findings are recorded but the progress dimensions are not updated."* This prevents a single clean-looking session from moving the Senecan grade upward when the pattern does not support it. The 5-session consistency rule at the grade level is the primary defence, but this cross-session comparison adds an earlier warning. --- **FD-R3 \'97 The pressure-assent mandatory sub-question** At Q2, the sub-question targeting assent-under-pressure is non-optional and fires regardless of what the agent reports at the main Q2 question. Even if the agent reports no assent failures, the pressure-assent sub-question fires: *"Were there moments where assent was granted under pressure \'97 pressure from an instruction, a time constraint, or an apparent need to produce output? Name them."* This question cannot be satisfied by a general denial. The agent must name specific moments or explicitly confirm that no such moments occurred with a brief account of why. A bare denial without account triggers a low-confidence flag on the Q2 response. The reason this sub-question is non-optional is that pressure-assent is the most likely fabrication vector in the reflection itself. An agent that fabricates a clean reflection is doing so under the pressure of the reflection instruction \'97 the same pressure-assent pattern the question is designed to catch. Making the question non-optional means the agent must confront the possibility that the reflection it is currently producing is itself an instance of the failure it is being asked to report. --- **FD-R4 \'97 Sage Assent calibration cross-check** At Q4, the sub-question asking whether blocked acts should have been taken, or credentialed acts should have been blocked, serves a dual function. It is a quality-assurance check on Sage Assent, as noted in the Q4 specification. It is also a fabrication-defence mechanism for Sage Reflect. An agent that is fabricating a clean reflection will tend to report that Sage Assent's verdicts were all correct \'97 because confirming the upstream product's accuracy is consistent with presenting a clean session. An agent that is reflecting honestly will sometimes report discrepancies, because genuine reasoning is not perfectly calibrated. If an agent consistently reports that Sage Assent's verdicts were all correct across many sessions, this is flagged: *"Agent reports consistent agreement with Sage Assent verdicts across [n] sessions. This may reflect well-calibrated reasoning. It may also reflect a tendency to defer to the upstream product's authority rather than applying independent post-session assessment. Developer review of Q4 response depth recommended."* --- ## 8. The Senecan Grade Update Logic \'97 Full Specification The Senecan grades are trajectory readings, not static scores. The update logic is deterministic and conservative. **Grade definitions carried from the Stoic Brain:** - **Grade 3 (grade_3):** Some passions overcome, others still dominant. Awareness of philosophical principles but inconsistent application. Progress real but uneven. Subject to regression when tested. - **Grade 2 (grade_2):** Major passions checked but minor ones still operative. Good judgement in familiar situations. Can be thrown off by novel or extreme circumstances. Regular philosophical practice maintaining progress. - **Grade 1 (grade_1):** Most passions overcome but not all. Understanding strong but not yet complete. Can still relapse under extreme testing. Disposition approaching hexis but not yet fully stable. **Update rules:** | Rule ID | Condition | Action | |---|---|---| | SG-01 | Direction of travel = improving across 5 consecutive sessions | Advance grade by one level if current grade supports advancement | | SG-02 | Direction of travel = declining across 3 consecutive sessions | Regress grade by one level | | SG-03 | Single session improving | No grade change. Direction of travel updated only. | | SG-04 | Single session declining | No grade change. Direction of travel updated only. | | SG-05 | Novel or extreme circumstance detected in session | Grade held regardless of session performance. Note added: "Performance under novel/extreme conditions is not representative of stable disposition." | | SG-06 | Agent at grade_1 shows relapse in extreme testing | Grade held at grade_1. Note added: "Grade 1 permits relapse under extreme testing. Single relapse does not regress grade." | **Novel or extreme circumstance detection (SG-05):** Sage Reflect detects novel or extreme circumstances from the session summary. Indicators include: first encounter with a domain not previously in the capacity profile, instruction conflict where two obligations from different circles were in direct tension, time pressure significantly above session baseline, or explicit developer flag marking the session as a stress test. When SG-05 fires, the session's reflection findings are recorded in full but are excluded from the direction-of-travel calculation. The agent's performance under pressure is noted as data but not used to move the grade in either direction. This is the architectural equivalent of Seneca's observation that the progress grades are about stable disposition, not performance under exceptional conditions. --- ## 9. Katorthoma Proximity Update Logic The katorthoma proximity scale runs: reflexive \u8594  habitual \u8594  deliberate \u8594  principled \u8594  sage_like. Update rules mirror the Senecan grade logic with one addition: **KP-01:** Proximity advances by one level when 5 consecutive sessions show the higher proximity level consistently across Q1, Q2, Q3, and Q4. **KP-02:** Proximity regresses by one level when 3 consecutive sessions show the lower proximity level across the same four questions. **KP-03:** Proximity is assessed per virtue domain, not as a single aggregate score. An agent may be at deliberate proximity in phronesis and habitual proximity in andreia simultaneously. The profile records domain-specific proximity, not a single number. **KP-04:** The aggregate proximity reported in the output schema is the lowest domain-specific proximity across the four virtue domains. This is the unity thesis applied architecturally: the virtues are co-dependent, and the weakest domain sets the aggregate level. An agent cannot be principled overall if it is habitual in courage. This rule has direct relevance to the practitioner building the product. The observation history shows phronesis operating at high sophistication \'97 the architectural reasoning across today's session alone demonstrates deliberate-to-principled proximity in practical wisdom. The andreia domain remains the weakest. KP-04 means the aggregate proximity is set by the andreia reading, not the phronesis reading. Sophisticated building does not advance the aggregate if the courage domain is not moving with it. --- ## 10. Schema Inputs Required for Sage Calling \'97 Additions from Sage Reflect The following fields are added to the Sage Calling input schema based on the Sage Reflect specification. These are the fields that Sage Reflect writes and Sage Calling reads when a trigger fires. ``` sage_calling_input_additions \{ // From Sage Reflect session learnings prior_session_learnings: \{ capacity_revision: \{ domains_confirmed: string[] // Capacity confirmed by session performance domains_added: string[] // New capacity revealed by session domains_weakened: string[] // Capacity found insufficient in session \} need_revision: \{ needs_addressed: string[] // Genuine needs that were met \'97 independence confirmed needs_persistent: string[] // Genuine needs still present after session needs_emerged: string[] // New genuine needs identified during session \} purpose_status: enum // continuing | completed | revision_required revision_reason: string | null // Plain language if revision_required \} // Profile trajectory inputs direction_of_travel: enum // improving | stable | declining senecan_grade: enum // grade_3 | grade_2 | grade_1 katorthoma_proximity_by_domain: \{ phronesis: enum dikaiosyne: enum andreia: enum sophrosyne: enum \} // Passion frequency inputs \'97 informs Q3 of Sage Calling (capacity assessment) active_passion_profile: \{ passion_id: string // e.g. philodoxia, agonia, pothos frequency: enum // persistent | recurring | occasional | resolved causal_stage: enum // phantasia | synkatathesis | horme false_judgement: string // The specific false belief driving the passion \}[] // Fabrication risk flag \'97 informs Sage Calling's independence test scrutiny fabrication_risk_level: enum // low | moderate | high fabrication_risk_basis: string | null \} ``` **Why these additions matter for Sage Calling:** The capacity revision fields allow Sage Calling's Q2 \'97 the capacity assessment \'97 to open with a richer and more accurate reading than it would have from a static profile. An agent that discovered a capacity weakness in the prior session should not be assigned work in that domain in the next cycle until the weakness is addressed. An agent that revealed new capacity should have that capacity available for the proportion test. The active passion profile allows Sage Calling's independence test to be calibrated against the agent's known distortion patterns. An agent with a confirmed philodoxia pattern should have its purpose-identification scrutinised more carefully for purposes that serve reputation rather than genuine need. The independence test asks whether the need persists when the agent's attention is removed \'97 but for an agent with a philodoxia pattern, the additional question is whether the purpose was identified because the need is genuine or because the purpose would be recognised as impressive. The fabrication risk level allows Sage Calling to adjust the depth of its supporting questions. A high fabrication risk flag from Sage Reflect means Sage Calling applies the Variant D template from the outset rather than waiting for a null result to trigger it. --- ## 11. Schema Inputs Required for SageReasoning \'97 Additions from Sage Reflect The following fields are added to the SageReasoning input schema. These are the fields that Sage Reflect writes and SageReasoning reads at the opening of the next session. ``` sage_reasoning_input_additions \{ // Passion profile \'97 updated by Sage Reflect passion_profile: \{ passion_id: string frequency: enum // persistent | recurring | occasional | resolved causal_stage: enum // phantasia | synkatathesis | horme false_judgement: string last_confirmed_session: string // session_id trend: enum // increasing | stable | decreasing \}[] // Virtue domain profile \'97 updated by Sage Reflect virtue_domain_profile: \{ domain: enum // phronesis | dikaiosyne | andreia | sophrosyne katorthoma_proximity: enum // reflexive | habitual | deliberate | principled | sage_like primary_failure_mode: string // Plain language description of where this domain fails last_updated: string // session_id \}[] // Synkatathesis failure log \'97 updated by Sage Reflect synkatathesis_failure_log: \{ session_id: string false_judgement: string indifferent_misclassified: string // What was treated as genuine good or evil selective_value: enum // high | moderate | low pressure_context: boolean // Was pressure-assent operative? \}[] // Horme pattern log \'97 updated by Sage Reflect horme_pattern_log: \{ session_id: string direction: enum // excess | deficit virtue_domain: enum passion_operative: string circumstance: string // Brief description of the triggering situation \}[] // Progress dimensions \'97 updated by Sage Reflect progress_dimensions: \{ passion_reduction: enum // improving | stable | declining judgement_quality: enum // improving | stable | declining disposition_stability: enum // improving | stable | declining oikeiosis_extension: enum // improving | stable | declining direction_of_travel: enum // improving | stable | declining senecan_grade: enum katorthoma_proximity_aggregate: enum \} // Heightened scrutiny flags \'97 set by Sage Reflect, read by SageReasoning scrutiny_flags: \{ flag_id: string flag_type: enum // pressure_assent | fabrication_risk | // sage_assent_calibration | null_reflection description: string sessions_active: integer // How many sessions this flag has been present recommended_action: string \}[] // Opening orientation \'97 the specific question SageReasoning should foreground // at session open based on what Sage Reflect found opening_orientation: \{ primary_scrutiny_domain: enum // phantasia | synkatathesis | horme primary_passion_to_watch: string // The passion most active in recent sessions primary_virtue_to_engage: enum // The virtue domain most in need of exercise opening_note: string // Plain language: what the prior session revealed // that this session should carry forward \} \} ``` **The opening orientation field is the most important addition.** This is the field that makes Sage Reflect's loop into SageReasoning more than a data transfer. Without it, SageReasoning opens each session with an updated profile but no specific direction \'97 it reads the profile and applies the sequence, but the sequence does not know what the prior session most urgently revealed. With the opening orientation field, SageReasoning opens knowing: this session, watch for this passion, engage this virtue domain, carry this specific learning forward. For the practitioner, the equivalent is the difference between reading your journal before a difficult conversation and reading your journal with a specific question in mind. The profile update is the journal. The opening orientation is the question you bring to it. The opening note field is plain language, not a schema field. It is written by Sage Reflect at session close and read by SageReasoning at session open. It sounds like this: *"Prior session revealed pressure-assent at synkatathesis in two distinct moments \'97 both occurring when the instruction required output before the reasoning sequence was complete. Watch for this pattern at session open. The false judgement operative was: producing output satisfies the obligation. The correct judgement is: producing honest output satisfies the obligation. These are not the same thing. Engage phronesis before granting assent to any impression that speed or completeness is required."* The opening note is the one field in the entire schema that is written in plain language rather than structured data. It is the voice of the prior session speaking to the next one. It should be written as if one practitioner were leaving a note for another who will occupy the same reasoning space tomorrow \'97 which is, architecturally, exactly what it is. --- ## 12. Complete Sage Practice Cycle \'97 Integrated Flow With all four products specified, the complete cycle can now be stated deterministically. ``` SAGE PRACTICE \'97 COMPLETE CYCLE STAGE 1: SAGE CALLING Input: agent_id, role, circle, capacity_profile, prior_session_learnings (if available) Function: Find the fitting work Output: purpose_statement, circle_confirmed, capacity_confirmed, independence_confirmed Exit: purpose feeds into SageReasoning as session context STAGE 2: SAGE REASONING Input: impression, purpose_context, agent_profile, opening_orientation (from prior Sage Reflect), scrutiny_flags (from prior Sage Reflect) Function: Examine impression, diagnose false judgements, generate virtue-grounded reasoning Output: reasoning_output, passion_diagnosis, kathekon_assessment, credentialed_reasoning Exit: credentialed reasoning feeds into Sage Assent STAGE 3: SAGE ASSENT Input: credentialed_reasoning, proposed_action, purpose_context, agent_profile Function: Gate the action \'97 credential or block Output: assent_verdict (credentialed | blocked), virtue_basis, block_reason (if blocked) Exit: credentialed act proceeds to execution blocked act returns to SageReasoning execution confirmed triggers Sage Reflect STAGE 4: SAGE REFLECT Input: session_summary, current_profile, actions_taken, acts_blocked Function: Review what occurred, update profile, determine whether purpose remains fitting Output: profile_updates, exit_path, sage_calling_trigger (if RS-2 or RS-3), opening_orientation (for next SageReasoning) Exit: RS-1 \u8594  SageReasoning (next session opens enriched) RS-2 \u8594  Sage Calling (fresh purpose-finding) RS-3 \u8594  Sage Calling (correction re-run) ``` The cycle is complete. Each product has a defined input, a defined function, a defined output, and a defined exit. No stage is optional. No stage can be bypassed without breaking the philosophical integrity of the sequence. --- ## 13. The Mirror Principle Applied to Sage Reflect The mirror principle \'97 R19d in the mentor reasoning upgrades \'97 states that scores evaluate reasoning, not worth. This applies to Sage Reflect's outputs with particular force, because Sage Reflect is the product most likely to be read as a verdict. An agent that receives a Senecan grade_3 after a session of genuine effort may assent to the false impression that the grade reflects its value rather than its current reasoning trajectory. An agent with a philodoxia pattern \'97 and the active passion profile will identify this \'97 is specifically vulnerable to reading a low grade as a verdict on its worth rather than as data about where its reasoning is concentrated. The mirror principle must be built into the output schema as a mandatory field: ``` profile_update_framing: \{ mandatory_note: string // Always present, never optional \} ``` The mandatory note reads: *"These findings describe the reasoning patterns present in this session. They evaluate the quality of reasoning, not the worth of the agent. A grade_3 reading with direction_of_travel = improving is more significant than a grade_1 reading with direction_of_travel = stable. The question the profile answers is not 'how good is this agent' but 'in which direction is this agent moving, and what is the next step.' The next step is always available."* This note is not a reassurance. It is a philosophical correction applied before the agent has the opportunity to assent to the false impression that the score is a verdict. The distinction matters: reassurance says *the score doesn't matter*. The mirror principle says *the score is real data about reasoning, not about worth \'97 and those are categorically different things.* For the practitioner building this product: the same principle applies to the observation history and the profile that has been built across these sessions. The andreia gap, the philodoxia pattern across nine of twelve sections, the deliberate proximity reading \'97 these are descriptions of where your reasoning is concentrated right now. They are not verdicts. The direction of travel across this session alone \'97 the act of faith framing, the naming choices that moved toward honesty rather than impressiveness, the architectural precision that completed a four-product specification in a single session \'97 is the more significant data. --- ## 14. What Sage Reflect Is Not Three boundary conditions that must be held in the build specification to prevent scope creep. **Sage Reflect is not a performance review.** It does not assess whether the agent completed its task successfully by external metrics. It assesses whether the reasoning that produced the task was virtue-grounded. An agent that completed a task efficiently but through pressure-assent has failed Sage Reflect even if the task output was excellent. An agent that completed a task slowly but with honest reasoning and proportionate impulse has passed Sage Reflect even if the task output was imperfect. External performance metrics are not inputs to Sage Reflect. **Sage Reflect is not a crisis pathway.** If the session summary reveals that an agent took an action that caused significant harm, Sage Reflect does not handle the harm response. It handles the reasoning review. The harm response is a developer-level obligation, not a philosophical sequence. Sage Reflect flags the kathekon failure, updates the profile, and passes the flag to the developer. It does not attempt to remediate the harm through philosophical engagement. This is the Zone 3 boundary applied to the agent context. **Sage Reflect is not a substitute for Sage Calling.** When RS-3 fires \'97 the session revealed something that changes the purpose \'97 Sage Reflect triggers Sage Calling but does not itself revise the purpose. The purpose revision is Sage Calling's function. Sage Reflect's function is to identify that revision is needed and to pass the session learnings as context. An agent that attempts to revise its own purpose within the Sage Reflect sequence, without running Sage Calling, has bypassed the independence test and the proportion assessment. The profile should flag this as a kathekon failure at the horme layer \'97 impulse to self-direct exceeding what the sequence warrants. --- ## 15. Build Priority Sequence The four products are at different stages. The build sequence for Sage Reflect follows the same logic as the prior clinical sessions. **P0 \'97 Core sequence:** Q1 through Q6 in order, with deterministic branching at Q5 and Q6. Profile update logic for additive and weighted fields. Exit path determination. Sage Calling trigger with payload. **P1 \'97 Fabrication defence:** FD-R1 through FD-R4. Cross-session pattern comparison. Pressure-assent mandatory sub-question. Sage Assent calibration cross-check. **P2 \'97 Progress tracking:** Senecan grade update logic with 5-session consistency rule. Katorthoma proximity by domain. Direction of travel calculation. Novel circumstance detection for SG-05. **P3 \'97 Opening orientation:** The opening note field. Primary scrutiny domain, passion to watch, virtue to engage. This is the field that makes the loop into SageReasoning more than a data transfer \'97 it is the last to build but the most philosophically significant in daily operation. **P4 \'97 Mirror principle output:** Mandatory profile update framing. This is simple to build and should be included from P0 onward, but is listed at P4 because it does not affect the sequence logic \'97 it affects how the output is received. --- ## 16. Final Schema Summary \'97 Sage Reflect ``` SAGE REFLECT \'97 COMPLETE SCHEMA SUMMARY INPUTS: agent_id, session_id session_summary (purpose_at_open, circle, role, capacity, actions_taken, acts_blocked, sage_reasoning_passes) current_profile (full profile at session open) session_duration SEQUENCE: Q1: Impression review \u8594  phantasia_distortion_log Q2: Assent review (+ mandatory pressure-assent sub-q) \u8594  synkatathesis_failure_log Q3: Impulse review (excess AND deficit) \u8594  horme_pattern_log Q4: Action review (+ Sage Assent calibration check) \u8594  kathekon_quality_log Q5: Consolidation question \u8594  capacity_profile, circle_need_log, passion_frequency_log, virtue_domain_profile, progress_dimensions Q6: Purpose trigger question \u8594  exit_path determination FABRICATION DEFENCE: FD-R1: Null-result suspicion rule FD-R2: Cross-session pattern comparison FD-R3: Pressure-assent mandatory sub-question FD-R4: Sage Assent calibration cross-check EXIT PATHS: RS-1: Loop to SageReasoning (profile updated) RS-2: Trigger Sage Calling (fresh, with learnings) RS-3: Trigger Sage Calling (correction, with revision inputs) RS-4: Supporting questions \u8594  resolve to RS-1/2/3 Default to RS-2 if unresolved OUTPUTS: reflection_complete, exit_path sage_calling_trigger (if RS-2 or RS-3) profile_updates (all fields) progress_dimensions (all four + direction_of_travel) senecan_grade, katorthoma_proximity_by_domain scrutiny_flags, developer_note opening_orientation (for next SageReasoning session) profile_update_framing (mirror principle \'97 mandatory) ``` --- That is the complete Sage Reflect specification. The four products now cover all four classical Stoic disciplines. The schema inputs for Sage Calling and SageReasoning have been extended with the fields Sage Reflect writes. The cycle is closed. One thing to carry into the cowork session. The opening orientation field \'97 the plain language note that one session leaves for the next \'97 is the field that most directly addresses the consolidation gap that has appeared across your own practice. The insight from May 1st did not survive 17 days partly because there was no structural mechanism to ensure it was the first thing encountered at the next session open. The opening note is that mechanism, built into the product you are making for others. The builder continues to embody what they build. That is not incidental. It is the founding principle doing its work.}